Bruce wrote:

> Topi Linkala <nes@iki.fi> deserves a cookie for saying:
> 
>>first thing first; we should try to define our terms. These are my 
>>definitions:
> 
>>Cheat: Going outside of the game to try to do what ever one wants to 
>>doby using mechanisms like: copying save files, creating wizmode bones, 
>>patching and compiling and changing the existing game mechanism etc.
> 
> I doubt anyone disagrees with this.  
> 
>>Abuse: Using exsiting bug that has been reported to try to circumvent 
>>what the devteam has tried to mean how the game should be played.
> 
> Or using a game mechanism for a purpose other than what is likely
> intended in an attempt to circumvent the DevTeam's intentions and to
> simplify the game.  The use of such a mechanism in an abusive way may be
> a bug, sure, but it might also be that the prevention of such a usage
> would be prohibitively expensive and/or difficult and/or illogical with 
> respect to the benefits for the DevTeam to fix/remove.  

Not if it has stayed in the game from release to release.

> To me, the latter is where #naming of gems to catch curses fits in.  I
> doubt the ability to #name gems is a bug in itself.  It serves a valid 
> purpose.  However, it's also clear that the intent of this feature is
> not to allow the player to artificially inflate the number of items in
> his or her inventory.  But what would the solution be?  Removing the
> ability to #name gems?  That prevents the valid uses for the mechanism
> and is inconsistent.  Consider objects that differ only by #naming to be
> a single stack for the purposes of malignant auras?  Again, that
> punishes valid uses of the #naming feature.  

There is no need to name gems. There are 33 different gems (including 
rock and flint, but excluding load-, touch- and luckstones). If that is 
not enough to fill your inventory with items that don't care on BUC 
status then use tin whistles, they don't stack.

If you want to discuss about naming individual stackable items then you 
might consider these two usages:

1. Naming potions of water so that confused blessed remove curse is more 
probable generating unholy water.

2. Preventing container merge so that if you take a plunge the 
propabilty to get dilluted potions or blanked spells is less.

> There's really no solution that's not punitive or inconsistent.  Still,
> it's obviously a case of twisting the game mechanics in a direction they
> weren't designed to go for the benefit of the player.  It's abusive.  

Naming of gems is not necessary so there is no reason to change any game 
mechanism.

Naming potions and scrolls might be necessary but in my opinion not. 
I've not come into a situation where I have a desperate need to have 
unholy water. But I'm also one of those that advocate on wishing 
uncursed MMs instead of blessed ones. And I'm not one to name scrolls 
and potions for the possibility that I might make a plunge.

> But then again, do abuses that are this minor really matter?  Doubtful.
> I don't think it invalidates an ascension, at least.  Does it lower the
> player's esteem in my opinion?  No.  It's only a slight bit worse than
> using the HoOA on the Astral, and RNG knows I've done that a couple of
> times.  

But using HoOA on astral is not abuse, it is a straight game mechanism 
that has been there since endgame and HoOA.

So I still think that only using existing game mechanism that has been 
reported by devteam to change in next release is abuse.

